Step 1: Explain the problem set:
- Attention spans are under attack, and the amount of time kids spend on a screen before moving on has decreased from 150 to 47 seconds
- The challenge was set out for us: How do we turn the existing Sundar Gutka into an experience that nurtures attention towards Gurbani for children?

Step 2: Enter the application as a kid
- Choose the character that resonates the most with you... in the future, we'll allow you to take a photo of your face and create your own custom character
- The next screen is your home screen - shows you where you're at with your current baani, how many Nanak coins you've accrued, and what your next step might be. 

Step 3: Enter Japji Sahib
- Enter the world of Japji Sahib, and start with whatever Pauri you'd like. Stars are earned and accrued once you are able to say a pauri and pronounce it 
well
- Each group of pauris is set up as a new world, so that the child feels like they're advancing as they continue their journey towards mastery

Step 4: Enter Pauri 1
- Entering a pauri, there are two main approaches to gaining mastery: Practice (practicing your pronounciation and receiving feedback) and Challenge (measure 
yourself and build mastery on the journey to collecting stars and Nanak coins)
